[FINAL-TEXT] Direct evidence found. One device flagged with matching DROP fingerprint. Advancing to Phase 5.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The `iptables` FORWARD chain on router6 contains three blanket DROP rules that together block all forwarded traffic arriving on its eth2 interface (the WAN-facing link to router3 via `r6_r3`).

**Mechanism:** The three rules are:
- `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`
- `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`
- `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`

Any packet that enters router6 through eth2 from the core routing fabric (via router3) and needs to be forwarded onward to the 172.18.51.x subnet (through mswitch2/switch10) is silently dropped before it exits. This creates a one-way "gate": traffic from the 172.18.51.x side can leave through eth2 (output direction, not matched by `-i eth2`), but replies and all inbound traffic from the rest of the network are discarded. This explains the timeout behavior seen from pc4 and pc5 trying to reach pc18 — packets reach router6 and are silently dropped.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, input interface eth2.

**Root cause:** Three iptables DROP rules on router6's FORWARD chain for interface eth2 block all forwarded traffic entering from the core network, effectively isolating the 172.18.51.x subnet from the rest of the topology.

**Fix:** Remove the three rules on router6:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```
=== END ===